=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The reported failure is real — Location A (PC4/PC5/Laptop0) cannot reach Location
C or any other non-directly-connected subnet — but the stated cause is wrong. The
WAN link between router0 (eth2, 10.16.1.1) and router1 (eth2, 10.16.1.2) is
physically UP and L3-reachable: ping across it returns 0% packet loss at sub-ms
latency. The actual fault is on router0's routing-protocol plane.

The file /etc/frr/frr.conf is missing entirely on router0 — it does not exist on
disk. Because ripd requires this file to load its configuration at startup, ripd
crashes immediately and shows "FAILED" in frr service status; watchfrr also
reports FAILED. Only zebra, mgmtd, and staticd are still running. Consequently,
router0's FRR running-config is completely empty: there is no "router rip" block,
no network statements, and no protocol configuration of any kind.

Without ripd, router0 neither advertises its connected subnets into the RIP domain
nor learns routes from the rest of the topology. Its RIB contains only directly
connected prefixes plus two static kernel entries. When PC4 tries to reach PC11 in
Location C, the packet reaches router0 but router0 has no route to the destination
and immediately returns an ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — confirming the
forwarding failure originates at router0 itself.

Root cause: The /etc/frr/frr.conf configuration file on router0 was deleted during
the maintenance window, causing ripd to fail and leaving router0 with no dynamic
routing.

Corrective fix: Restore /etc/frr/frr.conf on router0 with the appropriate RIP
configuration (matching the rest of the domain: router rip, network statements
covering router0's interfaces — at minimum network 10.0.0.0/8 to include the WAN
link and any additional network statements for 172.16.0.0/12 and 192.168.0.0/16
subnets), ensure the file is owned by frr:frr with correct permissions, then
restart FRR with "service frr restart".
=== END ===
